For a more in-depth treatments of the topic of regular variation, with specific focus on useful results we refer the reader to (Bingham et al. 1987) For statistical applications of regular variation, see (Nair et al. 2022; Emchrechts et al. 2012; de Haan and Ferrerira 2006). Many classical results applying this theory to probablity, particularly the arcsin distribution and applications to renewal theory can be found in Volume II of Feller (Feller 1971).

Preliminary Definitions

Definition 1 A function \(f : \R \rightarrow \R\) is regularly varying at \(\infty\) with exponent \(\alpha \in \R\), and write \(f \in \calR_\alpha^{\infty}\) if for all \(\lambda &gt; 0\), \[
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{x\uparrow\infty}\frac{f(\lambda x)}{f(x)} = \lambda^{\alpha}.
\end{equation*}
\]


Definition 2 A function \(\ell : \R \rightarrow \R\) is slowly varying at \(\infty\), and write \(\ell \in \calR_0^{\infty}\), if for all \(\lambda &gt; 0\), \[
\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{x\uparrow\infty}\frac{\ell(\lambda x)}{\ell(x)} = 1.
\end{equation*}
\]


Definition 3 We call \(g : \R \rightarrow \R\) regularly varying at \(0\) with exponent \(\alpha \in \R\), and write \(g \in \calR_\alpha^{0}\) if for all \(\lambda &gt; 0\), \[
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{s\downarrow 0}\frac{g(\lambda s)}{g(s)} = \lambda^{\alpha}.
\end{equation*}
\]

Structurally, this is equivalent to a regularly varying function at infinity with reciprocal argument, i.e., \[
\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{s\downarrow 0}\frac{f\pr{\frac{1}{\lambda s}}}{f\pr{\frac{1}{s}}} = \lambda^{-\alpha} .
\end{equation*}
\] By construction, we can define any regularly varying function as the product of a slowly varying function \(\ell \in \calR_{0}^{\infty}\) and a power law part \(x^{\alpha} \in \calR_{\alpha}^{\infty}\). That is, if \(f \in \calR_{\alpha}^{\infty}\), we may write \[
\begin{equation*}
    f(x) = \ell(x) x^{\alpha},
\end{equation*}
\] where \(\ell(x)\) is a suitably-chosen slowly varying function that captures deviation of \(f(x)\) from a pure power law \(\propto x^{\alpha}\) at finite values of \(x\).1 2
1 It is useful to consider slowly varying functions \(\ell(x)\) as sub-polynomial (Lemma 2.7 in Nair et al. 2022), where \[\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{x \uparrow \infty} \ell(x)x^{\rho} = \begin{cases}
        0, \quad \rho &lt; 0 \\
        \infty, \quad \rho &gt; 0.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}\] Two simple examples of slowly varying functions are constants \(\ell(n) = C &gt; 0\) and \(\ell(n) = \pr{\log n}^\gamma\) for any \(\gamma \in \R\).2 It is also very important here to ask what this means with respect to regularly varying sequences \(f_n\). Here there is no burden, and we may just define \(f_n = (f_n/n^{\alpha})n^{\alpha} \doteq \ell(n) n^{\alpha}\), though we must restrict ourselves to \(n\in \Z_{+}\); for instance by taking the integer part \(f(x) =\ell([x])[x]^\alpha\). What however is interesting, is that we may go even further and consider a continuous argument \(x\in \R_+\) by defining a continuous function \(f(x)\) which agrees with \(f_n = f(n)\) on the integers, and otherwise has useful properties. For instance, for a regularly varying sequence \(f_n\) we can simply define \(f(x)\) as its smooth interpolation such that \(f([x]) = f_{[x]}\). This representation allow us to often ignore whether our regularly varying function is continuous or discrete, and derive general results.

Fundamental Theorems
The first of the ``fundamental theorems of regular variation’’ (for derivatives) to be discussed is the Monotone Density Theorem (Theorem 2.11 in (Nair et al. 2022)). This and the accompanying theorem to follow (dealing with integrals; see Theorem 2.10 in (Nair et al. 2022) or Theorem A3.7 in (Emchrechts et al. 2012)) will be used frequently in our proofs to follow. The condition of eventual-monotonicity required for the Monotone Density theorem is in some cases difficult to check, but is essentially determined by the behavior of the slowly varying function \(\ell(\cdot)\). This condition is often evaluated with a secondary notion, for instance log-concavity \(\mu_{j}^2\ge \mu_{j-1}\mu_{j+1}\) for all \(j &gt; N\). As such, monotonicity is often treated as a simplifying assumption, or is ensured by selection of a specific case.

Theorem 1 Let \(F \in \calR_{\alpha}^{\infty}\) and let \(\Delta F\) denote the derivative \(\Delta F(n) = F'(n)\), if \(F\) is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure, and the finite difference operator \(\Delta F(n) = F(n)-F(n-1)\) if \(F\) is discrete. If \(\Delta [F]\) is eventually monotone in the sense that there exists an \(N\) such that for \(\Delta F(n)\) is monotone for \(n &gt; N\), then \[
\begin{align*}
    n \Delta F (n) \sim \alpha F(n), \quad n &gt; N,
\end{align*}
\] and therefore \(\Delta F \in \calR_{\alpha-1}^{\infty}\).


We consider the discrete case, which is not often seen, but whose proof is similar to the absolutely continuous case, where one needs only to substitute an integral whenever a sum appears below. Without loss of generality, assume that \(\Delta [F] (n)\) is non-decreasing for \(n &gt; N\), and let $0 &lt; a &lt; b $; the non-increasing will be implied by changing the direction the following inequalities. By definition of \(F\), we can of course write \[
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i = [sn]}^{[tn]} \frac{\Delta [F] (n)}{F (n)} = \frac{F ([tn]) - F ([sn])}{F (n)}.
\end{equation*}
\] With $n &gt; N / s $, by monotonicity we have \[
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\Delta [F] ([sn])([tn] - [sn])}{F(n)} \le \frac{F ([tn]) - F ([sn])}{F (n)} \le \frac{\Delta [F] ([tn])([tn] - [sn])}{F(n)} .
\end{equation}
\tag{1}\] By regular variation, as \(F \in \calR_{-\alpha}\), we may represent \(F(n) = \ell(n)n^{-\alpha}\). Considering only valid integer arguments we see \[
\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{n}\frac{F ([sn])}{F (n)} = \lim_{n}\frac{[sn]^{\alpha}\ell([sn])}{n^{\alpha}\ell(n)} = s^{\alpha}.
\end{equation*}
\] The ratio \(\lim_{n}[sn]/n = s\), is well known result, and therefore of course this implies that \(\lim_{n}\pr{[sn]/n}^{\alpha} = s^{\alpha}\). When \(\ell(\cdot)\) is eventually non-decreasing as supposed by eventual monotonicity of \(F(\cdot)\), then \[
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\ell(sn-1)}{\ell(n)} \le \frac{\ell([sn])}{\ell(n)} \le \frac{\ell(sn)}{\ell(n)} \rightarrow 1
\end{align*}
\] the left side can be re-written and satisfies the same limit as the right expression in our string of inequalities \[
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\ell(sn-1)}{\ell(n)}  = \frac{\ell((s-\frac{1}{n})n)}{\ell(n)} \rightarrow 1.
\end{align*}
\] If \(\ell(\cdot)\) is not continuous, but rather a discrete \(\ell_{[n]}\), as we will discuss later it is equivalent to define a new eventually monotone function which continuously interpolates \(\ell([n])=\ell_{[n]}\) and use it as a surrogate. Going back now to Equation 1, dividing all terms by \(n(t-s)\), consider the left most expression \[
\begin{equation*}
    \limsup_{n}\frac{n\Delta [F] ([sn]) }{F(n)}\frac{([tn] - [sn])}{n(t-s)}.
\end{equation*}
\] The second term in the product simply tends to \(1\) \[
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{([tn] - [sn])}{n(t-s)} \rightarrow 1,
\end{equation*}
\] so that in total \[
\begin{equation*}
    \limsup_{n}\frac{n \Delta [F] ([sn]) }{F(n)} \le \frac{t^{\alpha} - s^{\alpha}}{t-s}
\end{equation*}
\] By considering the right most expression in Equation Equation 1 we also have that \[
\begin{equation*}
    \liminf_{n}\frac{n\Delta [F] ([tn]) }{F(n)} \ge \frac{t^{\alpha} - s^{\alpha}}{t-s}.
\end{equation*}
\] where letting \(a=1\) (respectively \(b=1\)) and taking \(b\downarrow1\) (respectively \(a\uparrow1\)) gives the desired result that by squeeze \[
\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{n}\frac{n \Delta [F] (n) }{F(n)} = \alpha.
\end{equation*}
\]

Theorem 1 demonstrates that, far enough into the tail, the contribution of \(\Delta F\) to \(F\) can be estimated by simply taking the derivative of its power-law part. In the case of a probability distribution, this result indicates that when the tail is regularly varying, i.e., \(\Fbar(m) = \Pr[S_1 &gt; m]=\ell(m)m^{-\alpha}\), then so long as its probability masses \(f_m\) are eventually-monotone decreasing, \(f_m \sim \alpha \ell(m) m^{-(1+\alpha)}\).
There are two important differences to highlight between the absolutely continuous case and the discrete case of Theorem 1. First is whether we express the original function \(F\) as an integral or a summation when considering the ratio \[
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i = [sn]}^{[tn]} \frac{\Delta F (n)}{F (n)} = \frac{F ([tn]) - F ([sn])}{F (n)}.
\end{equation*}
\] Second is whether \(\ell(\cdot)\) is continuous, rather discrete (i.e., \(\ell(n) = \ell_{[n]}\)). In the discrete case, as we will discuss later, it is equivalent to define a new eventually monotone function which smoothly interpolates \(\ell([n])=\ell_{[n]}\).
The complement to Theorem 1 is known as Karamata’s Integral Theorem. While the Monotone Density Theorem states that \(\Delta F(x) \sim \ell(x) \Delta(x^\alpha)\) as \(x\uparrow\infty\), Karamata’s Integral Theorem states that \(\int_0^y F(x)\dd x \sim \ell(y) \int_0^{y}x^\alpha \dd x\) as \(y\uparrow\infty\) (up to some dependence on \(\alpha\)). As this is no longer a point-wise result and instead requires integration over an interval, we will need the following theorem indicating the uniformity by which regular variation applies. A proof can be found in (de Haan and Ferrerira 2006), where this result appears as Theorem B.1.4.

Theorem 2 If \(f \in \calR_{\alpha}^{\infty}\) then there exists a \(t_0 \ge 0\) such that for any bounded and non-negative \(a\) and \(b\), \[
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{f(xt)}{f(x)} \rightarrow t^{\alpha}, \quad \text{uniformly over} \  t \in [a,b], \  t_0 \le a &lt; b &lt; \infty  .
\end{equation*}
\]


Theorem 3 Let \(f \in \calR_{\alpha}^{\infty}\), then there exists a \(t_0 &gt; 0\) such that, \[
\begin{equation*} \begin{aligned}
    \int_{t_0}^{t}f(y) \dd y &\sim \frac{1}{\alpha + 1}tf(t), \quad t \uparrow \infty
        \quad \alpha \ge -1, \\
    \int_{t}^{\infty}f(y) \dd y &\sim \frac{-1}{(\alpha + 1)}tf(t), \quad t \uparrow \infty
    \quad \alpha &lt; -1.
\end{aligned} \end{equation*}
\] That is, \(\int f(y) \dd y \in \calR_{\alpha + 1}^{\infty}\) for \(\alpha &lt; 0\) and \(\alpha &gt; 0\), respectively, over their valid intervals.

A proof of Theorem Theorem 3 can be found in the appendix of (de Haan and Ferrerira 2006), as Theorem B.1.5 (see also Theorem 2.10 in (Nair et al. 2022) or Theorem A3.7 in (Emchrechts et al. 2012)). The primary argument comes from applying the Uniform Convergence Theorem for regular varying functions after a change of variables \[
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{F(tx)-F(t)}{tf(t)} = \int_{1}^{x}\frac{f(tu)}{f(t)} \dd u \rightarrow \frac{x^{\alpha + 1}-1}{\alpha + 1}, \quad t \uparrow \infty.
\end{equation*}
\] Extending this result to include integration over \([0,x]\) or \([x,\infty)\) for \(\alpha &gt; -1\) and \(\alpha \le -1\), respectively, requires integrability of \(f\) over these regions so that the denominator \(tf(t)\) dominates \(\int f(s)\dd s\) over these regions.
